[{:db/id "6333ef2c-1cae-519e-a685-58c87c26695f" :answer/id #uuid "6333ef2c-1cae-519e-a685-58c87c26695f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "da65e013-dd31-5b49-8080-62718daaad6d" :answer/id #uuid "da65e013-dd31-5b49-8080-62718daaad6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "a3f4a2a2-bf74-5d31-bfe7-5cd3ea7de0d5" :answer/id #uuid "a3f4a2a2-bf74-5d31-bfe7-5cd3ea7de0d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "f574aa17-b6bd-5157-b731-6ae70b45cf9d" :answer/id #uuid "f574aa17-b6bd-5157-b731-6ae70b45cf9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c570990c-5907-5cdf-82b5-65a7360aed17" :answer/id #uuid "c570990c-5907-5cdf-82b5-65a7360aed17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "99cc235e-b8c3-5f58-9f50-751a34cdf8ea" :answer-field/id #uuid "99cc235e-b8c3-5f58-9f50-751a34cdf8ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["6333ef2c-1cae-519e-a685-58c87c26695f" "da65e013-dd31-5b49-8080-62718daaad6d" "a3f4a2a2-bf74-5d31-bfe7-5cd3ea7de0d5" "f574aa17-b6bd-5157-b731-6ae70b45cf9d" "c570990c-5907-5cdf-82b5-65a7360aed17"] :answer-field/correct "a3f4a2a2-bf74-5d31-bfe7-5cd3ea7de0d5" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "73587b1a-dcf1-5c8f-a281-fdffe625841b" :question/id #uuid "73587b1a-dcf1-5c8f-a281-fdffe625841b" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 2\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/8e/8ee9b491452a26902c4800a006c16f1bbca31f0e800ec9aad20dfeef68eed8f4.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 2\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (2\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}2\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}2\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}2\\cdot (r)^{2}((1)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}2\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ((3))^{3})-(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(18)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}18\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[18\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(36\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=36\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["99cc235e-b8c3-5f58-9f50-751a34cdf8ea"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "e9530bbf-c5c3-5952-9005-276940846bcc" :answer/id #uuid "e9530bbf-c5c3-5952-9005-276940846bcc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "b6ca9be8-5d2e-57ab-a777-f3c7100f1c02" :answer/id #uuid "b6ca9be8-5d2e-57ab-a777-f3c7100f1c02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "cd2021fe-3967-595c-8aa9-57ea29061bf4" :answer/id #uuid "cd2021fe-3967-595c-8aa9-57ea29061bf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "9e084867-80ed-5e99-995e-d2f7c3a2775a" :answer/id #uuid "9e084867-80ed-5e99-995e-d2f7c3a2775a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c0223973-2b8f-51d9-95d0-c1ac09f3ee98" :answer/id #uuid "c0223973-2b8f-51d9-95d0-c1ac09f3ee98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "dc2c7090-300d-5bba-8d56-8e59b2ef1c31" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc2c7090-300d-5bba-8d56-8e59b2ef1c31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["e9530bbf-c5c3-5952-9005-276940846bcc" "b6ca9be8-5d2e-57ab-a777-f3c7100f1c02" "cd2021fe-3967-595c-8aa9-57ea29061bf4" "9e084867-80ed-5e99-995e-d2f7c3a2775a" "c0223973-2b8f-51d9-95d0-c1ac09f3ee98"] :answer-field/correct "e9530bbf-c5c3-5952-9005-276940846bcc" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "267fd235-ecde-531c-b2aa-2c537397b3c6" :question/id #uuid "267fd235-ecde-531c-b2aa-2c537397b3c6" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3=3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (\\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}(r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}((3)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}3\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[(r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(((1))^{3})-(((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(1)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}1\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(2\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=2\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["dc2c7090-300d-5bba-8d56-8e59b2ef1c31"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "08dadd38-fb68-5779-a188-b784be4a6b2a" :answer/id #uuid "08dadd38-fb68-5779-a188-b784be4a6b2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "a91f9201-835b-5ae6-be79-7518add5a8bf" :answer/id #uuid "a91f9201-835b-5ae6-be79-7518add5a8bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "7d83eaae-f8b5-538e-af6a-fa57c9f45461" :answer/id #uuid "7d83eaae-f8b5-538e-af6a-fa57c9f45461" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c9be48c8-ddc2-53db-874a-8450e1fe1768" :answer/id #uuid "c9be48c8-ddc2-53db-874a-8450e1fe1768" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "6cbee069-7a87-51d0-9392-cbeec9ec586e" :answer/id #uuid "6cbee069-7a87-51d0-9392-cbeec9ec586e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "d30d0b97-29d6-5b4a-be3d-426bd7d15400" :answer-field/id #uuid "d30d0b97-29d6-5b4a-be3d-426bd7d15400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["08dadd38-fb68-5779-a188-b784be4a6b2a" "a91f9201-835b-5ae6-be79-7518add5a8bf" "7d83eaae-f8b5-538e-af6a-fa57c9f45461" "c9be48c8-ddc2-53db-874a-8450e1fe1768" "6cbee069-7a87-51d0-9392-cbeec9ec586e"] :answer-field/correct "c9be48c8-ddc2-53db-874a-8450e1fe1768" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "c0e834b8-fc6b-56b5-8146-fbd537f8a411" :question/id #uuid "c0e834b8-fc6b-56b5-8146-fbd537f8a411" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4=4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (4\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{4}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}((4)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}16\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot ((1))^{3})-(\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{16}{3})-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\frac{16}{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["d30d0b97-29d6-5b4a-be3d-426bd7d15400"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "ae54fd97-3a30-519e-9ebd-e1fb979dbe8a" :answer/id #uuid "ae54fd97-3a30-519e-9ebd-e1fb979dbe8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "3112b487-c350-57db-82e9-a721ed41fe97" :answer/id #uuid "3112b487-c350-57db-82e9-a721ed41fe97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/ensure :